  "pr_number": 8603,
  "issue_number": 8042,
  "issue_title": "x86_64-pc-cygwin: tokio::process stdout/stderr stop after the first chunk",
  "issue_body": "**Version**\n`tokio v1.51.1`\n\n**Platform**\n`CYGWIN_NT-10.0-19045 DESKTOP-KPFSK42 3.6.7-1.x86_64 2026-03-02 20:13 UTC x86_64 Cygwin`\n\n**Description**\n\nOn `x86_64-pc-cygwin`, `tokio::process` can stop delivering child stdout/stderr after the first chunk when stdio is\npiped and read asynchronously.\n\nThe child itself exits normally, but the async reader sees only the early output and then never sees the later output\nor EOF.\n\nThis seems specific to the Mio `poll` backend path. Reproduced on Cygwin, but root cause appears to be generic to Mio poll-backend + raw SourceFd process-pipe handling, so other poll-backend Unix targets may also be affected.\n\n##### Reproducer\n\n`Cargo.toml`:\n\n```toml\n[package]\nname = \"tokio-cygwin-process-repro\"\nversion = \"0.1.0\"\nedition = \"2024\"\n\n[dependencies]\nanyhow = \"1\"\ntokio = { version = \"1.51.1\", features = [\"macros\", \"rt-multi-thread\", \"process\", \"io-util\", \"time\", \"sync\"] }\n```\n\nsrc/main.rs:\n\n```rust\nuse std::process::Stdio;\nuse std::time::{Duration, Instant};\n\nuse anyhow::{Context, Result};\nuse tokio::io::AsyncReadExt;\nuse tokio::process::Command;\nuse tokio::sync::mpsc::{unbounded_channel, UnboundedSender};\n\nfn spawn_reader<R>(mut reader: R, sender: UnboundedSender<String>)\nwhere\n    R: tokio::io::AsyncRead + Unpin + Send + 'static,\n{\n    tokio::spawn(async move {\n        let mut buffer = [0u8; 8192];\n        loop {\n            match reader.read(&mut buffer).await {\n                Ok(0) => break,\n                Ok(read) => {\n                    let chunk = String::from_utf8_lossy(&buffer[..read]).into_owned();\n                    if sender.send(chunk).is_err() {\n                        break;\n                    }\n                }\n                Err(error) => {\n                    eprintln!(\"read error: {error:#}\");\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    });\n}\n\n#[tokio::main(flavor = \"multi_thread\")]\nasync fn main() -> Result<()> {\n    let shell = \"printf 'builtin1\\\\n'; /usr/bin/sleep 0.2; /usr/bin/printf 'external\\\\n'; printf 'builtin2\\\\n'\";\n\n    let mut child = Command::new(\"/bin/bash.exe\")\n        .args([\"-lc\", shell])\n        .stdin(Stdio::null())\n        .stdout(Stdio::piped())\n        .stderr(Stdio::null())\n        .spawn()\n        .context(\"spawn failed\")?;\n\n    let stdout = child.stdout.take().context(\"missing stdout\")?;\n    let (sender, mut receiver) = unbounded_channel::<String>();\n    spawn_reader(stdout, sender);\n\n    let mut combined = String::new();\n    let mut exit = None;\n    let started = Instant::now();\n\n    loop {\n        tokio::select! {\n            maybe = receiver.recv() => {\n                match maybe {\n                    Some(chunk) => combined.push_str(&chunk),\n                    None => break,\n                }\n            }\n            status = child.wait(), if exit.is_none() => {\n                exit = status?.code();\n            }\n            _ = tokio::time::sleep(Duration::from_millis(100)) => {\n                if exit.is_some() && started.elapsed() > Duration::from_secs(3) {\n                    break;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        if exit.is_some() && receiver.is_closed() {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n\n    println!(\"combined = {:?}\", combined);\n    println!(\"exit = {:?}\", exit);\n    Ok(())\n}\n```\n\n##### Expected result\n\nThe child stdout should be:\n\nbuiltin1\nexternal\nbuiltin2\n\nand the reader should see EOF after the child exits.\n\n##### Actual result on Cygwin\n\nOnly the first chunk is delivered:\n\ncombined = \"builtin1\\n\"\nexit = Some(0)\n\nThe later output (`external\\nbuiltin2\\n`) never arrives, and the async reader does not observe EOF within the timeout.\n\n##### Additional narrowing\n\nI tried to narrow this down further:\n\n- `std::process::Command` + blocking reader threads reads all output correctly.\n- `std::process::Command` + manual nonblocking `read()` retry loop also reads all output correctly.\n- Direct `mio::unix::SourceFd` on the same child stdout fd reproduces the same failure: one readable event, first chunk only.\n- If I explicitly call `registry.reregister(..., Interest::READABLE)` after each successful read with Mio, all output arrives correctly.\n\n##### Suspected cause\n\nThis appears to be an interaction between Tokio process pipes and Mio's `poll` backend on Cygwin.\n\nCygwin uses Mio's `poll` selector backend. That backend clears the delivered interest after an event and expects the FD to be re-armed later. Mio's `IoSource` path has a do_io mechanism that re-registers on WouldBlock, and Tokio Unix process pipes appear to use a raw `SourceFd` + direct `read` path instead.\n\nSo after the first successful read, there is no re-arm on this backend, and later child output is never observed.\n\nIf that reading is correct, this may affect `tokio::process` stdio on all targets that go through Mio's `poll` selector path, beside Cygwin.",
